{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '909,1024p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 909\"","aggregated_output":"   909\t### 7.3 Installing a module is a scheduler child task\n   910\t\n   911\tThe linker delivers a verified image with its obligations. Installing it\n   912\tis the scheduler's, as an explicit child evaluation with its own state:\n   913\t\n   914\t```mermaid\n   915\tstateDiagram-v2\n   916\t  [*] --> loading: 5b discharged\n   917\t  loading --> running: kernel loader accepted the image\n   918\t  loading --> refused: loader defect\n   919\t  running --> parked: child effect blocks (stream, ffi, require)\n   920\t  parked --> running: child wait satisfied\n   921\t  running --> validated: halt; every export bound in child store\n   922\t  running --> refused: halt; :export-missing, or error\n   923\t  validated --> linked: bindings published to registry value\n   924\t  linked --> [*]\n   925\t  refused --> [*]\n   926\t```\n   927\t\n   928\tRules:\n   929\t\n   930\t- **The child is a task, not a call.** The scheduler holds\n   931\t  `:installs {name {:phase p :vm child :waiters [task-ids] :parent\n   932\t  link-id}}`. The child is a VM of the same backend with a fresh,\n   933\t  isolated store containing nothing of any other task's state (UCF\n   934\t  section 7.6.2 applied to loading), its own wait-set, and the parent's\n   935\t  registry value as its module view. The scheduler steps it in its\n   936\t  ordinary round beside every other task; nothing about it runs inside\n   937\t  wait restoration.\n   938\t- **The requiring task waits on the install, not on the linker.** Once\n   939\t  the child starts, the parent's entry becomes\n   940\t  `{:reason :install :name 'foo}`, and any other task that requires\n   941\t  `'foo` meanwhile joins `:waiters`. No task is restored until the\n   942\t  install reaches `linked` or `refused`.\n   943\t- **`running` may park.** A module body that reads a stream, calls FFI,\n   944\t  or requires another module parks the child exactly as any task parks;\n   945\t  a transitive require emits its own link request under its own id\n   946\t  (section 7.4). The parent is parked on `:install` and is unaffected;\n   947\t  the scheduler keeps stepping every other task. There is nothing to\n   948\t  deadlock on except a cycle, which section 7.4 refuses.\n   949\t- **`validated` requires halt plus exports.** The child must reach halt\n   950\t  (not error, not an abandoned park), and every name under\n   951\t  `:yin.module/exports` must be a key of the child's store. A missing\n   952\t  export is `:export-missing`; an error is the error. Nothing from a\n   953\t  refused child's store is published.\n   954\t- **`linked` relocates the exports, then updates the registry.** A\n   955\t  child's store holds closures whose code coordinates are the child's:\n   956\t  a stack or register closure carries a body pc into the child's held\n   957\t  segment and no image identity; a semantic closure carries a local\n   958\t  segment id minted by the child's loader. Published as they are, those\n   959\t  coordinates are meaningless in the parent, so bindings cross the\n   960\t  child-to-parent boundary the way a continuation crosses any boundary:\n   961\t  by lift and lower. The `linked` transition is three acts in order.\n   962\t  1. *The parent loads the verified image* through its ordinary loader,\n   963\t     the same value the child loaded, so the parent's coordinate space\n   964\t     now contains it: the semantic VM mints fresh local ids and writes\n   965\t     the address to its alias column; the stack and register VMs append\n   966\t     with relocation by offset (`yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image` and\n   967\t     `append-register-image` are exactly this). An image already\n   968\t     present is not loaded twice; the alias column and the held\n   969\t     segment's identity say so.\n   970\t  2. *The child's export slice is lifted* to UCF's portable encoding\n   971\t     (section 7.5.1): each closure becomes a `:yin.k/closure` marker\n   972\t     with `:yin.k/segment` the image identity and `:yin.k/entry` the pc\n   973\t     relative to that image, with its captured environment encoded\n   974\t     recursively; every other value encodes by the same grammar. The\n   975\t     lift is refused as `:yin.k/non-portable` for the same leaves UCF\n   976\t     refuses (a host object, an in-memory handle), and a refused lift\n   977\t     refuses the install: an export that cannot cross a boundary is not\n   978\t     an export.\n   979\t  3. *The parent lowers the encoding* against its own coordinate space:\n   980\t     `:yin.k/segment` resolves through the alias column or the held\n   981\t     segment's offset table to the parent's local id or pc base, and\n   982\t     `:yin.k/entry` is added to that base. The lowered slice is what\n   983\t     the registry value gains as `:bindings`.\n   984\t  The registry value then gains `{'foo {:manifest m :address a\n   985\t  :derivation d :bindings lowered}}`, `resolve-var`'s module step reads\n   986\t  `:bindings`, and every waiter is restored with `'foo` as its value.\n   987\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\n   988\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\n   989\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\n   990\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\n   991\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\n   992\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process.\n   993\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\n   994\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\n   995\t  again.\n   996\t\n   997\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\n   998\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\n   999\tand rejected: it persists four descriptions of a fact that one\n  1000\tevaluation derives, and it would make the manifest's shape depend on\n  1001\twhich backends exist. This is *derive, don't persist* at the module\n  1002\tgrain.\n  1003\t\n  1004\t### 7.4 Transitive requires and cycles\n  1005\t\n  1006\tA module body may itself `(require 'bar)`. Under section 7.3 that is the\n  1007\tchild parking on its own `:link-request`, and it needs no further rule to\n  1008\tcomplete. One rule prevents deadlock: before starting a child for\n  1009\t`'bar`, the scheduler walks the install ancestry of the requiring task\n  1010\t(the chain of `:parent` link ids through `:installs`); if `'bar` is\n  1011\tinstalling anywhere on that chain, the require is `:require-cycle`\n  1012\tnaming the chain, and the child that asked is refused. A cycle is legal\n  1013\tonly when delivered as one unit by the linker's dependency closure.\n  1014\t\n  1015\tThe linker may resolve `:yin.module/requires` ahead of time from the\n  1016\tmanifest and deliver every image of the closure in one response, ordered\n  1017\tso that dependencies precede dependents and a strongly connected\n  1018\tcomponent arrives as one unit. This is Phase B7's dependency closure\n  1019\t(`yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md` D5, D6); this document fixes only that the\n  1020\tresponse is a vector of `[manifest derivation image obligations]`\n  1021\tentries in install order, that the scheduler starts one child per entry\n  1022\tin that order, and that a cycle is one unit hashed with canonical\n  1023\tmember ordering. How SCC identity is minted stays open (section 12).\n  1024\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '596,763p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 596\"","aggregated_output":"   596\t## 6. Linking is a stream exchange\n   597\t\n   598\t### 6.1 The topology\n   599\t\n   600\t```mermaid\n   601\tflowchart LR\n   602\t  subgraph VM[\"yin.vm scheduler (any backend)\"]\n   603\t    K[park on :link wait entry]\n   604\t  end\n   605\t  subgraph LNK[\"yin.vm.linker interpreter\"]\n   606\t    L[link-state / step]\n   607\t  end\n   608\t  subgraph SRV[\"content server boundary\"]\n   609\t    J[serve-content! over a jing handle]\n   610\t    D[(mem / file / dht handle)]\n   611\t    J --- D\n   612\t  end\n   613\t  K -- \"link request stream\" --> L\n   614\t  L -- \"link response stream\" --> K\n   615\t  L -- \"content request stream\" --> J\n   616\t  J -- \"content response stream\" --> L\n   617\t```\n   618\t\n   619\tFour streams, two pairs. The VM and the linker share the link pair; the\n   620\tlinker and the content server share the content pair. Each pair is\n   621\twired by the composition, and its transport is the composition's choice:\n   622\t\n   623\t| Composition           | Link pair    | Content pair    | Behind server |\n   624\t|-----------------------|--------------|-----------------|---------------|\n   625\t| single process, tests | ring buffer  | ring buffer     | mem handle    |\n   626\t| durable local         | ring buffer  | ring buffer     | file handle   |\n   627\t| remote content        | ring buffer  | ws + rpc        | remote's      |\n   628\t| peer network          | ring buffer  | ring buffer     | dht handle    |\n   629\t\n   630\tNothing in the linker or the VM changes across rows. This is I3 made\n   631\toperational: a \"local\" require and a \"remote\" require are the same four\n   632\tappends and four reads.\n   633\t\n   634\tThe content pair is `dao.jing.remote`'s existing wire vocabulary:\n   635\t`:jing/get-content` answering `{:found? boolean :value v}` over\n   636\t`dao.stream.rpc`. The linker writes no protocol of its own for content.\n   637\t\n   638\t**The DHT sits behind the served boundary.** `dao.jing.dht`'s handle is\n   639\ta synchronous `dao.jing` handle over `IDhtNet`, not a stepped DaoStream\n   640\tendpoint. It is not made one here. It is placed where the file and memory\n   641\thandles are: behind `serve-content!`, whose handler calls the handle. The\n   642\tlinker side is unchanged, and the server side is driven by whoever drives\n   643\tthe server today. A portable stepped DHT client would be a `dao.jing.dht`\n   644\tdesign and is out of scope; the peer-network row is therefore equivalent\n   645\tat the linker's boundary and no further, and M3's tests say so (section\n   646\t9).\n   647\t\n   648\t### 6.2 Linker-local state\n   649\t\n   650\t`link-state` is constructed once by the composition with everything the\n   651\tlinker needs to answer requests, none of which travels on a stream:\n   652\t\n   653\t```clojure\n   654\t(link-state {:rpc          rpc-state          ; dao.stream.rpc client\n   655\t             :formats      {format record}    ; section 5\n   656\t             :indexes      {format index}     ; identity -> address\n   657\t             :name-env     name-env           ; section 8.2, at snapshot\n   658\t             :authority    {...}              ; section 8.2\n   659\t             :bounds       {:max-parts n :max-depth d :max-bytes b}\n   660\t             :derivation   :verifying | :trusted     ; section 8.1\n   661\t             :fallback     :none | :h-for-r})        ; section 5.5\n   662\t```\n   663\t\n   664\tFunctions (`:formats`, an index given as a function) live here and only\n   665\there. A composition that wants a remote name environment or index\n   666\tobserves it into this state through an ordinary `dao.space` read over a\n   667\tstream the linker holds a cursor on; step 1 consults only what has been\n   668\tobserved.\n   669\t\n   670\t### 6.3 The stepped interface\n   671\t\n   672\tFollowing `dao.stream.md` OD-5 and the shape `dao.jing.remote.step`\n   673\talready has, the portable linker is a pure function over explicit state.\n   674\t\n   675\t```clojure\n   676\t(link-state opts)               ; -> state; no socket, atom, or scheduler\n   677\t(request-link state request)    ; -> [state link-id]\n   678\t(step state budget)             ; -> {:state s :completions [...]\n   679\t                                ;     :diagnostics [...]}\n   680\t(abandon state link-id reason)  ; -> state\n   681\t```\n   682\t\n   683\tA `request` is plain data and nothing else. Its closed key set is\n   684\t`:yin.link/id`, `:yin.link/format`, `:yin.link/contract` (optional), and\n   685\texactly one of `:yin.link/name` or `:yin.link/identity`. The two\n   686\tadmissible shapes:\n   687\t\n   688\t```clojure\n   689\t;; by module name: the linker resolves the manifest (section 8)\n   690\t{:yin.link/id       [:t0 7]                  ; section 7.2, step 3\n   691\t :yin.link/format   :yin.debruijn.code\n   692\t :yin.link/contract \"b1\"\n   693\t :yin.link/name     'my.lib}\n   694\t\n   695\t;; by bare identity: B6's closed-image path, no manifest\n   696\t{:yin.link/id       [:t0 8]\n   697\t :yin.link/format   :yin.debruijn.code\n   698\t :yin.link/contract \"b1\"\n   699\t :yin.link/identity H}\n   700\t```\n   701\t\n   702\tA request carrying both `:yin.link/name` and `:yin.link/identity`, or\n   703\tneither, is `:invalid-request`, as is any key outside the set or any\n   704\tvalue that is not a scalar, keyword, symbol, address, or the id vector.\n   705\tA request carries no index, no receiver, no function, and no handle. The\n   706\trefusal is appended on the response stream under the request's id where\n   707\tthe id is well formed, and under `:yin.link/id nil` otherwise; it is\n   708\tnever processed further. The receiver's capabilities do not travel\n   709\tbecause discharge happens at the receiver (section 4.2, 5b).\n   710\t\n   711\t`step` advances every in-flight link in a fixed order: re-attempt\n   712\tunsent content requests, poll the content response medium at most\n   713\t`budget` elements, route each correlated response to its link, run the\n   714\tverification step that response enables, issue the next content request\n   715\tthe worklist needs (a part, under `:parts-fn`), and take completions.\n   716\tEach link is a small state machine over the six steps; steps 3 to 5a\n   717\tnever touch a stream and run the moment step 2 completes.\n   718\t\n   719\tA completion is the step-6 result or a refusal, tagged with its link id:\n   720\t\n   721\t```clojure\n   722\t{:yin.link/id 7 :status :ok :manifest m :derivation d :image {...}}\n   723\t{:yin.link/id 7 :status :refused :reason :absent :address a}\n   724\t```\n   725\t\n   726\tA link with no completion yet is `:pending`; the caller steps again\n   727\twhen it chooses. Retry cadence, deadlines, and permanent-absence policy\n   728\tare the composition's, as D6 states: streams report, they do not decide.\n   729\t\n   730\t`abandon` retires a link's bookkeeping and completes it `:lost` with the\n   731\tgiven reason, so a caller that gives up still receives exactly one\n   732\tcompletion and never reuses the id.\n   733\t\n   734\t### 6.4 `fetch` is host policy over an explicit runtime\n   735\t\n   736\tThe B6 signature changes in its first argument:\n   737\t\n   738\t```clojure\n   739\t(fetch runtime format identity)\n   740\t(fetch runtime format identity receiver)\n   741\t```\n   742\t\n   743\t`runtime` is `{:state link-state :drive (fn [state] state')}`. `:drive`\n   744\tis the composition's driver: it steps the linker's client side *and*\n   745\twhatever serves the content pair (`dao.stream.rpc/serve-once!` over the\n   746\tcontent handle, or nothing when a remote server runs elsewhere), and it\n   747\tis called in a loop until the one link completes. `fetch` therefore\n   748\tholds no `dao.jing` handle and cannot call `jing/get`: the content\n   749\thandle is reachable only from the server side of the content pair. A\n   750\ttest in M3 asserts this by wiring a server whose handle counts `get`\n   751\tcalls and a linker state with no handle at all, and by checking that\n   752\tevery local `fetch` produces exactly the request/response traffic on the\n   753\tcontent pair that the stepped path produces. `fetch` runs step 5b\n   754\tagainst the `receiver` argument before returning.\n   755\t\n   756\t`verify`, the pure steps 3 to 5a over values already in hand, and\n   757\t`discharge`, the pure step 5b over obligations and a receiver, are both\n   758\texported so tests and compositions holding a payload can run the checks\n   759\twithout a stream.\n   760\t\n   761\tNo new interpreter may be written against `fetch`. The VM path of\n   762\tsection 7 is written against `step`.\n   763\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '764,908p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 764\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '764,908p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | nl -ba -v 764\"","aggregated_output":"   764\t## 7. `(require 'foo)` lowers to the linker\n   765\t\n   766\t### 7.1 Today\n   767\t\n   768\t`yin.vm/primitives` binds `require` to an effectful primitive that\n   769\treturns `{:effect :module/require :module ns-sym}`.\n   770\t`yin.vm.engine/handle-effect` dispatches it to the registry's effect\n   771\thandler, and `yin.vm.module/require-handler` answers from\n   772\t`(:modules state)` alone: present, the module name; absent, a thrown\n   773\t`ex-info`. The registry was populated by `register-module` at wiring\n   774\ttime with host functions. This is the \"static ad-hoc in-memory\n   775\tdictionary lookup\" the critique names. Its two defects: a module that is\n   776\tnot in the dictionary cannot be obtained at all, and the dictionary holds\n   777\tcode (host functions) rather than naming it.\n   778\t\n   779\t### 7.2 The unified flow\n   780\t\n   781\t```mermaid\n   782\tsequenceDiagram\n   783\t  participant P as program\n   784\t  participant S as VM scheduler\n   785\t  participant L as yin.vm.linker\n   786\t  participant J as content server (over dao.stream)\n   787\t  P->>S: (require 'foo)\n   788\t  S->>S: :module/require effect\n   789\t  alt manifest already linked in registry value\n   790\t    S-->>P: 'foo\n   791\t  else not linked\n   792\t    S->>S: mint response cursor; :link-request entry\n   793\t    S->>L: append link request {id, name 'foo, format f}\n   794\t    S->>S: append ok -> :link-response entry\n   795\t    L->>L: name env at snapshot: 'foo -> one manifest\n   796\t    L->>J: get-content manifest address\n   797\t    J-->>L: manifest (verified; name checked)\n   798\t    L->>J: get-content derivation record, image(s)\n   799\t    J-->>L: image (verified, 6 steps; derivation checked)\n   800\t    L->>S: append link response {id, :ok image, obligations}\n   801\t    S->>S: match id; discharge obligations (5b)\n   802\t    S->>S: child task: loading -> running -> validated\n   803\t    S->>S: registry value += {'foo manifest, bindings}\n   804\t    S-->>P: 'foo\n   805\t  end\n   806\t```\n   807\t\n   808\tThe steps, as rules:\n   809\t\n   810\t1. **The effect is unchanged.** `require` still returns\n   811\t   `{:effect :module/require :module 'foo}`. Nothing upstream of the\n   812\t   engine learns anything.\n   813\t2. **The handler checks the registry value first.** A manifest already\n   814\t   linked in `(:modules state)` answers now, as `next` answers `ok` when\n   815\t   the value is there. This is not the dictionary lookup surviving: the\n   816\t   registry holds manifests and bindings produced by an earlier child\n   817\t   evaluation, or by the composition's host-module declaration (section\n   818\t   8.3). A module currently installing (section 7.3) is neither linked\n   819\t   nor absent: the requiring task joins that install's waiter set.\n   820\t3. **A miss mints a cursor, then parks in the request state.** In this\n   821\t   order: the handler mints a `:dao.stream/newest` cursor on the link\n   822\t   response stream; then it builds the wait entry in its first state:\n   823\t\n   824\t   ```clojure\n   825\t   {:reason    :link-request\n   826\t    :link-id   [:t0 7]                 ; [origin counter], below\n   827\t    :envelope  {:yin.link/id [:t0 7] :yin.link/name 'foo\n   828\t                :yin.link/format :yin.debruijn.code\n   829\t                :yin.link/contract \"b1\"}\n   830\t    :request   {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\n   831\t    :response  {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\n   832\t    :cursor    <the kept cursor minted above>}\n   833\t   ```\n   834\t\n   835\t   Minting before appending is the `dao.stream.md` *Cursors* rule for\n   836\t   observing events caused by an operation: a response that lands\n   837\t   before the cursor exists would otherwise be skipped. The entry is\n   838\t   registers, resource ids, and plain data only; no handle and no\n   839\t   closure is attached.\n   840\t\n   841\t   **Link ids are scoped to the link pair, not to a VM.** Several VMs\n   842\t   share one response stream: the root task and every install child\n   843\t   (section 7.3) each have their own engine and their own `:id-counter`,\n   844\t   so a counter alone collides. A link id is therefore the pair\n   845\t   `[origin counter]`: `origin` is a task origin tag the scheduler that\n   846\t   owns the link pair mints from its own counter when it creates a task\n   847\t   (the root task at start, each child at `loading`), unique among every\n   848\t   task that has ever written to that pair; `counter` is the task's own\n   849\t   engine gensym. The scheduler never reuses an origin tag and a task\n   850\t   never reuses a counter, so no two requests on one pair share an id.\n   851\t   A composition that multiplexes several schedulers onto one pair must\n   852\t   give each scheduler a distinct origin prefix as composition data;\n   853\t   two schedulers on one pair with one prefix is a host assembly defect.\n   854\t4. **The request is appended; `ok` moves the entry to the response\n   855\t   state.** The handler appends `:envelope` through the link request\n   856\t   writer the composition supplied in `opts`. On `ok` the entry becomes\n   857\t   `{:reason :link-response :link-id 7 :response ... :cursor ...}`. On\n   858\t   `full` the entry stays in `:link-request` with its envelope retained\n   859\t   verbatim and is retried on the next poll, the `:ffi-request`\n   860\t   discipline. The two states are the two UCF pending variants this\n   861\t   flow adds (section 11, item 10): `:link-request` carries everything\n   862\t   needed to rebuild the envelope, `:link-response` carries the response\n   863\t   descriptor and the kept cell.\n   864\t5. **The linker interpreter observes the request stream** with its own\n   865\t   cursor, in its own control flow. It resolves the name through its\n   866\t   name environment at the snapshot it was composed with (section 8.2),\n   867\t   fetches the manifest by address through the `:yin.module/manifest`\n   868\t   format record so the manifest itself passes steps 2 to 4, checks the\n   869\t   manifest's declared name (section 8.1), selects the derivation and\n   870\t   identity for the requested format, verifies the derivation, and runs\n   871\t   the six steps for the image through the stepped core. A manifest with\n   872\t   no derivation for the requested format is `:unsupported-format`, and\n   873\t   the composed `:fallback` policy (section 5.5) applies, under the\n   874\t   composed `:derivation` policy, before a refusal is appended.\n   875\t6. **The response is appended** to the link response stream with the\n   876\t   request's exact `:yin.link/id`, as `:ok` with the image, manifest,\n   877\t   derivation, and obligations, or as the refusal. The linker holds\n   878\t   nothing afterwards.\n   879\t7. **`check-wait-set` polls each `:link-response` entry's kept cursor and\n   880\t   restores only on exact correlation.** On each poll the entry advances\n   881\t   past every response whose `:yin.link/id` is not its own, keeping its\n   882\t   cursor at the first unconsumed position, and acts only on a response\n   883\t   whose id equals `:link-id`. This is the per-cell round-order\n   884\t   discipline UCF section 7.5.3 already gives shared cursors, so a\n   885\t   parked `:link-response` lifts and lowers as any other wait does.\n   886\t   Handling of every other case is fixed:\n   887\t   - *duplicate*: a second response for an id whose entry has already\n   888\t     restored is skipped; ids are `[origin counter]` pairs minted once\n   889\t     and never reused, so the entry cannot exist again;\n   890\t   - *late*: a response for an id whose entry was abandoned is skipped;\n   891\t   - *unknown*: a response for an id no entry holds is skipped;\n   892\t   - *abandoned*: a composition that gives up on a link removes the\n   893\t     entry, calls `abandon` on the linker side, and raises the reason to\n   894\t     the program as the effect's error; the id is retired.\n   895\t   Each skip emits a telemetry diagnostic and consumes nothing but the\n   896\t   cursor position. On a matching `:ok` the scheduler runs step 5b\n   897\t   against its live state and, if discharged, starts the install child\n   898\t   (section 7.3). On a matching refusal, or a failed discharge, the\n   899\t   program resumes with the refusal raised as the effect's error, the\n   900\t   same way an `:ffi` `error` raises. On `gap` the honest outcome is a\n   901\t   `gap`, and the composition's policy decides.\n   902\t\n   903\tRevision r1 claimed steps 3 to 7 needed no new engine machinery. That\n   904\twas wrong in two places: the wait entry needs the two-state shape above,\n   905\tand installation needs the child task below. Park entries, kept cursors,\n   906\t`handle-stream-block`, and `check-wait-set` are reused; `:link-request`,\n   907\t`:link-response`, and the install child are new.\n   908\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"The new ID scheme and step-0 contract check are explicit. The remaining question is whether the proposed export relocation has a defined representation for all four VMs; I’m comparing its UCF marker against the actual stack and register closure shapes."}}
