{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '250,405p;535,580p;838,875p;950,995p;1025,1105p;1120,1190p;1478,1548p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '250,405p;535,580p;838,875p;950,995p;1025,1105p;1120,1190p;1478,1548p'\"","aggregated_output":"   250\t```clojure\n   251\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.code      ; the dispatch key\n   252\t :contract            \"b1\"                    ; contract revision name\n   253\t :identity-fn         (fn [value] identity)   ; mint: publish! only\n   254\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [identity value] boolean) ; step 3\n   255\t :row-defect-fn       (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 2, per part\n   256\t :validate-fn         (fn [value] defect-or-nil)    ; step 4, whole\n   257\t :obligations-fn      (fn [value] [sym ...])        ; step 5a: free names\n   258\t :definitions-fn      (fn [value] #{sym ...})       ; step 5a: own defs\n   259\t :parts-fn            (fn [value] [address ...])}   ; step 2 worklist\n   260\t```\n   261\t\n   262\t- `:identity-fn` mints an identity from a payload. It is used by\n   263\t  `publish!` and never by verification.\n   264\t- `:identity-matches-fn` is the step-3 check, directed by the identity.\n   265\t  For H and R it is `(= identity (image-hash value))`, a contract-pinned\n   266\t  hash with one algorithm. For the storage-derived formats it is\n   267\t  `(jing/segment-matches? identity value)`, so a value minted under\n   268\t  `:sha256` while the default is `:blake3` verifies under the algorithm\n   269\t  the identity names and never fails spuriously.\n   270\t- `:row-defect-fn` validates one fetched part on its own, before any\n   271\t  address it references is enqueued: tag, arity, slot kinds, saturation.\n   272\t  For single-payload formats it is the whole validator, run early.\n   273\t- `:parts-fn` returns the storage addresses a fetched payload references\n   274\t  and without which it is incomplete. For the three single-payload\n   275\t  formats it returns nothing. For `:yin.ast/code` it returns the child\n   276\t  row ids of a row body.\n   277\t- `:obligations-fn` returns the free names of a value.\n   278\t  `:definitions-fn` returns the constant store keys the value itself\n   279\t  binds at module level. The linker subtracts the second from the first\n   280\t  and joins the remainder with the manifest's declarations to form\n   281\t  obligations (step 5a). For the AST record both are Datalog over the\n   282\t  rows (`yin.vm/free-names` and the store-key query behind\n   283\t  `yin.vm/ast-requirements`); for the vector formats both are operand\n   284\t  scans (`:var` against `:store-put`, `:load-free` against the\n   285\t  `yin/def` call sites).\n   286\t\n   287\t`fetch` and `step` contain no branch on `:format`. Adding a fifth backend\n   288\tis one more record.\n   289\t\n   290\t### 4.2 The six steps\n   291\t\n   292\tThe order is B6's, unchanged, and remains load-bearing for the reasons\n   293\tB6 gives. An admission check precedes step 1; step 2 is a bounded\n   294\tworklist; steps 1 and 2 are stream exchanges (section 6); step 5 has a\n   295\tlinker half and a receiver half.\n   296\t\n   297\t```text\n   298\t(link runtime format identity)\n   299\t\n   300\t0. admission     request malformed                  -> :invalid-request\n   301\t                 no format record for the format    -> :unsupported-format\n   302\t                 request contract differs from the record's\n   303\t                                                    -> :contract-mismatch\n   304\t1. address    <- (index identity)               linker-local index\n   305\t                 absent entry                       -> :absent\n   306\t2. payload    <- fetch address over dao.stream\n   307\t                 absent payload                     -> :absent\n   308\t                 (not (segment-matches? address v)) -> :address-mismatch\n   309\t                 ((:row-defect-fn format) v)        -> :descriptor-defect\n   310\t                 bounds exceeded                    -> :parts-limit\n   311\t                 enqueue ((:parts-fn format) v); repeat until empty\n   312\t3.               (not ((:identity-matches-fn format) identity root))\n   313\t                                                    -> :hash-mismatch\n   314\t4.               ((:validate-fn format) whole) returns a defect\n   315\t                                                    -> :descriptor-defect\n   316\t5a.              obligations <- join free names with declarations\n   317\t                 free name with no declaration      -> :undeclared-free\n   318\t5b.  (receiver)  each obligation discharged by exact equality\n   319\t                 profile or manifest differs        -> :unresolved-free\n   320\t                 name bound in free env or store    -> :shadowed-free\n   321\t6. return the verified image as data\n   322\t```\n   323\t\n   324\tStep by step, for all four formats:\n   325\t\n   326\t0. **Admit the request.** Before any lookup or fetch, the linker checks\n   327\t   the request's shape against the closed key set of section 6.3\n   328\t   (`:invalid-request`), that it holds a format record for the requested\n   329\t   format (`:unsupported-format`), and that the request's\n   330\t   `:yin.link/contract`, when present, equals the record's `:contract`\n   331\t   (`:contract-mismatch`). The contract check runs here, ahead of step\n   332\t   2's row-local grammar check, so no validator ever runs under the\n   333\t   wrong table; revision r2 placed it in step 4, after row-local\n   334\t   validation had already run, which was an ordering defect. For a\n   335\t   manifest-delivered image the same check runs again against\n   336\t   `:yin.module/contract` immediately after the manifest is verified\n   337\t   and before any derivation or image is fetched (section 8.1).\n   338\t1. **Resolve identity to storage address.** The index is linker-local\n   339\t   composition data: a map, or datoms `[identity attribute address]`\n   340\t   read through `index-from-datoms` from a `dao.space` source the linker\n   341\t   observes with its own cursor. An entry that has not been observed\n   342\t   yet is `:absent` now, which is the honest answer at this moment; a\n   343\t   later request may find it. Step 1 never waits and never queries a\n   344\t   remote index inline. For the storage-derived formats the identity is\n   345\t   its own address and the index is the identity function.\n   346\t2. **Fetch payload over `dao.stream`.** Each address is a\n   347\t   `:jing/get-content` request on the content request stream and a\n   348\t   correlated response on the content response stream (section 6). The\n   349\t   value is verified against the address it was requested at, using the\n   350\t   algorithm the address carries (`jing/segment-matches?`). Then, before\n   351\t   any child is enqueued, `:row-defect-fn` validates this part alone;\n   352\t   a malformed row is refused here and its slots are never followed.\n   353\t   Then `:parts-fn` names further addresses; each is fetched and\n   354\t   verified the same way; the worklist is breadth-first from the root\n   355\t   and visits each address once. The composition supplies bounds in the\n   356\t   runtime: `:max-parts`, `:max-depth`, `:max-bytes`; exceeding any is\n   357\t   `:parts-limit` naming the bound and the address at which it was hit.\n   358\t   The result of step 2 is the root value plus, for a multi-part\n   359\t   format, the map of every fetched part by address.\n   360\t3. **Verify format identity.** `(:identity-matches-fn format)` over the\n   361\t   requested identity and the root value. For H and R this is a\n   362\t   different hash under a different preimage from step 2 and catches a\n   363\t   stale or swapped index entry, a descriptor disagreement, or a\n   364\t   contract version disagreement (all `:hash-mismatch`, as in B6). For\n   365\t   the storage-derived formats it is `segment-matches?` under the\n   366\t   identity's own algorithm; it still runs, because it is the only check\n   367\t   that the *index* did not point the identity at a different, correctly\n   368\t   stored payload.\n   369\t4. **Validate structural and liveness invariants.** The format's\n   370\t   validator runs over the complete value: for a tree, over the\n   371\t   assembled row set (`:id-resolves`, `:acyclic`, `:root-reachable` are\n   372\t   whole-tree rules that step 2's row-local check cannot run); for a\n   373\t   vector, over the vector; for a register image, including the\n   374\t   ascending, bounded, exact live-set rules. A validator that throws is\n   375\t   `:descriptor-defect` with rule `:validator-refused`.\n   376\t5. **Verify dependency closure.** Two halves.\n   377\t   - *5a, at the linker: obligations.* Two scans run over the verified\n   378\t     value, and the second is subtracted from the first.\n   379\t     `:obligations-fn` yields the free names. `:definitions-fn` yields\n   380\t     the **definition set**: every store key the image itself binds at\n   381\t     module level, which is the section 7.7 store-key query of\n   382\t     `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` (`:vm/store-put` rows, `:store-put`\n   383\t     operands, `yin/def` applications with a constant key) restricted to\n   384\t     constant keys. A module that reads `x` after `(def x 1)` therefore\n   385\t     owes nothing for `x`: the name is bound in the module's own\n   386\t     obligation environment, and it is never flagged. Each remaining\n   387\t     free name is joined with the manifest that delivered the image\n   388\t     (section 8.1): a name under `:yin.module/primitives` becomes\n   389\t     `{:name n :kind :primitive :profile addr}`; a name exported by a\n   390\t     module under `:yin.module/requires` becomes\n   391\t     `{:name n :kind :module :module m :manifest addr}`. A free name\n   392\t     the manifest declares nowhere is `:undeclared-free`; the publisher\n   393\t     did not say what the name means, so no receiver can bind it\n   394\t     identically. The definition set also gives an early export check:\n   395\t     an export under `:yin.module/exports` that is not in the definition\n   396\t     set is `:export-missing` here, before any child runs; the runtime\n   397\t     check of section 7.3 remains for definitions the static scan cannot\n   398\t     see (a key computed at run time). An image requested by bare\n   399\t     identity with no manifest has no declarations; it verifies only if\n   400\t     it has no free names outside its definition set, which is B6's\n   401\t     closed-image case and remains supported.\n   402\t   - *5b, at the receiver: discharge.* Obligations travel with the\n   403\t     verified image in the response. The receiver checks each against\n   404\t     its live state as the first act of installation, before any load:\n   405\t     a `:primitive` obligation is discharged only by a primitive of\n   535\tscans `:var` operands not bound by an enclosing closure's params, the\n   536\t`$code` query of section 7.7; the linker module defines it, as B6\n   537\tdefined the two de Bruijn scanners. The contract stamp is UCF's\n   538\t`{:yin.code/contract \"v2\"}`; a link request for this format carries the\n   539\tstamp, and the receiver's index is keyed per stamp (UCF section 7.3.3).\n   540\tLoading is `yin.vm.semantic/load-vector`, which writes the address to the\n   541\timage and the alias column.\n   542\t\n   543\t### 5.3 `:yin.debruijn.code` (the stack VM)\n   544\t\n   545\t```clojure\n   546\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.code\n   547\t :contract            \"b1\"\n   548\t :identity-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash\n   549\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [H v] (= H (yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-hash v)))\n   550\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   551\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-code/image-defect\n   552\t :obligations-fn      stack-free-names\n   553\t :definitions-fn      stack-definitions\n   554\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   555\t```\n   556\t\n   557\tUnchanged from B6 section 5.1 in substance. The identity H is\n   558\t`image-hash` over the descriptor hash (which embeds the lowering contract\n   559\tversion) and the canonical vector; the `=` here compares two values of\n   560\tone contract-pinned hash and is not a Class 3 site. Free names are\n   561\t`:load-free` operands. Loading is `yin.vm.debruijn.stack/load-image`;\n   562\t`yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image` shows the relocation a second image\n   563\tneeds beside a held one.\n   564\t\n   565\t### 5.4 `:yin.debruijn.register` (the register VM)\n   566\t\n   567\t```clojure\n   568\t{:format              :yin.debruijn.register\n   569\t :contract            \"r1\"\n   570\t :identity-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   571\t :identity-matches-fn (fn [R v]\n   572\t                        (= R (yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-hash\n   573\t                               v)))\n   574\t :row-defect-fn       yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   575\t :validate-fn         yin.vm.debruijn-register-code/register-image-defect\n   576\t :obligations-fn      register-free-names\n   577\t :definitions-fn      register-definitions\n   578\t :parts-fn            (constantly nil)}\n   579\t```\n   580\t\n   838\t   registers, resource ids, and plain data only; no handle and no\n   839\t   closure is attached.\n   840\t\n   841\t   **Link ids are scoped to the link pair, not to a VM.** Several VMs\n   842\t   share one response stream: the root task and every install child\n   843\t   (section 7.3) each have their own engine and their own `:id-counter`,\n   844\t   so a counter alone collides. A link id is therefore the pair\n   845\t   `[origin counter]`: `origin` is a task origin tag the scheduler that\n   846\t   owns the link pair mints from its own counter when it creates a task\n   847\t   (the root task at start, each child at `loading`), unique among every\n   848\t   task that has ever written to that pair; `counter` is the task's own\n   849\t   engine gensym. The scheduler never reuses an origin tag and a task\n   850\t   never reuses a counter, so no two requests on one pair share an id.\n   851\t   A composition that multiplexes several schedulers onto one pair must\n   852\t   give each scheduler a distinct origin prefix as composition data;\n   853\t   two schedulers on one pair with one prefix is a host assembly defect.\n   854\t4. **The request is appended; `ok` moves the entry to the response\n   855\t   state.** The handler appends `:envelope` through the link request\n   856\t   writer the composition supplied in `opts`. On `ok` the entry becomes\n   857\t   `{:reason :link-response :link-id 7 :response ... :cursor ...}`. On\n   858\t   `full` the entry stays in `:link-request` with its envelope retained\n   859\t   verbatim and is retried on the next poll, the `:ffi-request`\n   860\t   discipline. The two states are the two UCF pending variants this\n   861\t   flow adds (section 11, item 10): `:link-request` carries everything\n   862\t   needed to rebuild the envelope, `:link-response` carries the response\n   863\t   descriptor and the kept cell.\n   864\t5. **The linker interpreter observes the request stream** with its own\n   865\t   cursor, in its own control flow. It resolves the name through its\n   866\t   name environment at the snapshot it was composed with (section 8.2),\n   867\t   fetches the manifest by address through the `:yin.module/manifest`\n   868\t   format record so the manifest itself passes steps 2 to 4, checks the\n   869\t   manifest's declared name (section 8.1), selects the derivation and\n   870\t   identity for the requested format, verifies the derivation, and runs\n   871\t   the six steps for the image through the stepped core. A manifest with\n   872\t   no derivation for the requested format is `:unsupported-format`, and\n   873\t   the composed `:fallback` policy (section 5.5) applies, under the\n   874\t   composed `:derivation` policy, before a refusal is appended.\n   875\t6. **The response is appended** to the link response stream with the\n   950\t  (not error, not an abandoned park), and every name under\n   951\t  `:yin.module/exports` must be a key of the child's store. A missing\n   952\t  export is `:export-missing`; an error is the error. Nothing from a\n   953\t  refused child's store is published.\n   954\t- **`linked` relocates the exports, then updates the registry.** A\n   955\t  child's store holds closures whose code coordinates are the child's:\n   956\t  a stack or register closure carries a body pc into the child's held\n   957\t  segment and no image identity; a semantic closure carries a local\n   958\t  segment id minted by the child's loader. Published as they are, those\n   959\t  coordinates are meaningless in the parent, so bindings cross the\n   960\t  child-to-parent boundary the way a continuation crosses any boundary:\n   961\t  by lift and lower. The `linked` transition is three acts in order.\n   962\t  1. *The parent loads the verified image* through its ordinary loader,\n   963\t     the same value the child loaded, so the parent's coordinate space\n   964\t     now contains it: the semantic VM mints fresh local ids and writes\n   965\t     the address to its alias column; the stack and register VMs append\n   966\t     with relocation by offset (`yin.repl`'s `append-stack-image` and\n   967\t     `append-register-image` are exactly this). An image already\n   968\t     present is not loaded twice; the alias column and the held\n   969\t     segment's identity say so.\n   970\t  2. *The child's export slice is lifted* to UCF's portable encoding\n   971\t     (section 7.5.1): each closure becomes a `:yin.k/closure` marker\n   972\t     with `:yin.k/segment` the image identity and `:yin.k/entry` the pc\n   973\t     relative to that image, with its captured environment encoded\n   974\t     recursively; every other value encodes by the same grammar. The\n   975\t     lift is refused as `:yin.k/non-portable` for the same leaves UCF\n   976\t     refuses (a host object, an in-memory handle), and a refused lift\n   977\t     refuses the install: an export that cannot cross a boundary is not\n   978\t     an export.\n   979\t  3. *The parent lowers the encoding* against its own coordinate space:\n   980\t     `:yin.k/segment` resolves through the alias column or the held\n   981\t     segment's offset table to the parent's local id or pc base, and\n   982\t     `:yin.k/entry` is added to that base. The lowered slice is what\n   983\t     the registry value gains as `:bindings`.\n   984\t  The registry value then gains `{'foo {:manifest m :address a\n   985\t  :derivation d :bindings lowered}}`, `resolve-var`'s module step reads\n   986\t  `:bindings`, and every waiter is restored with `'foo` as its value.\n   987\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\n   988\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\n   989\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\n   990\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\n   991\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\n   992\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process.\n   993\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\n   994\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\n   995\t  again.\n  1025\t## 8. The manifest registry\n  1026\t\n  1027\t### 8.1 The module manifest\n  1028\t\n  1029\tA manifest is content: one map, materialized in `dao.jing`, named by its\n  1030\taddress. It replaces the `{sym fn}` dictionary entry.\n  1031\t\n  1032\t```clojure\n  1033\t{:yin.module/name        'my.lib\n  1034\t :yin.module/contract    \"v2\"\n  1035\t :yin.module/tree        :segment/...        ; the one canonical tree\n  1036\t :yin.module/derivations {:yin.semantic/code     :segment/...  ; record\n  1037\t                          :yin.debruijn.code     :segment/...  ; record\n  1038\t                          :yin.debruijn.register :segment/...} ; record\n  1039\t :yin.module/index       {H :segment/... R :segment/...}       ; optional\n  1040\t :yin.module/exports     #{'foo 'bar}\n  1041\t :yin.module/requires    {'other.lib :segment/...}             ; manifest\n  1042\t :yin.module/primitives  {'println :yin.k.pp/sha256-...}       ; profile\n  1043\t :yin.module/footprint   {:store-keys #{} :effects #{}}}       ; UCF 7.6.1\n  1044\t```\n  1045\t\n  1046\tRules:\n  1047\t\n  1048\t- **One tree.** `:yin.module/tree` is the root row id of the canonical\n  1049\t  AST the module was authored as. The `:yin.ast/code` image *is* this\n  1050\t  tree, so it has no derivation record.\n  1051\t- **One derivation record per lowered format.** Each value under\n  1052\t  `:yin.module/derivations` is the address of a content-addressed\n  1053\t  derivation record in the shape `yin.vm.ledger/derive-record` mints:\n  1054\t  `{:yin.ledger/op :derive :yin.ledger/input tree\n  1055\t  :yin.ledger/output identity :yin.ledger/function f\n  1056\t  :yin.ledger/profile {...}}`. The linker fetches the record (through a\n  1057\t  `:yin.ledger/record` format record: `segment-matches?` identity, a\n  1058\t  validator over these keys, no parts, no obligations) and checks\n  1059\t  `:yin.ledger/input` equals `:yin.module/tree` and `:yin.ledger/output`\n  1060\t  is the identity it then fetches. A record leading from a different\n  1061\t  tree, or to a different identity, is `:derivation-mismatch`. Four\n  1062\t  individually valid images from four different trees therefore cannot\n  1063\t  share a manifest, and a swapped image fails before load.\n  1064\t- **Contract before content.** Immediately after the manifest passes\n  1065\t  steps 2 to 4, `:yin.module/contract` is checked against the format\n  1066\t  record for the requested format (`:contract-mismatch`), before the\n  1067\t  name check and before any derivation or image is fetched (section\n  1068\t  4.2, step 0).\n  1069\t- **Verifying and trusting are two policies, and verifying never\n  1070\t  trusts.** The linker's `:derivation` policy is one of `:verifying` or\n  1071\t  `:trusted`, chosen by the composition; there is no policy under which\n  1072\t  a verifying link accepts a claim it did not recompute.\n  1073\t  - Under `:verifying`, for every image it delivers the linker fetches\n  1074\t    the manifest's tree through the `:yin.ast/code` record (steps 2 to\n  1075\t    4, so the tree itself is verified), re-lowers it under the exact\n  1076\t    per-format profile the derivation record names (`\"ast-to-bytecode\"`\n  1077\t    for `:yin.semantic/code`, the B2 stack lowering for\n  1078\t    `:yin.debruijn.code`, the R1 register lowering for\n  1079\t    `:yin.debruijn.register`, each pinned by its own\n  1080\t    `:yin.ledger/profile` map), and compares the recomputed identity to\n  1081\t    `:yin.ledger/output` (`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 5.2.2, step\n  1082\t    2). Inequality is `:derivation-mismatch`. If the linker does not\n  1083\t    implement that exact profile, or the tree is `:absent`, or the tree\n  1084\t    fails its own verification, the link is refused\n  1085\t    `:unverified-derivation` naming the profile and what was missing.\n  1086\t    It is never downgraded to trust.\n  1087\t  - Under `:trusted`, the linker checks only the record's input and\n  1088\t    output addresses (the rule above) and reports `:trust :composition`\n  1089\t    in the outcome, exactly as B6's trusted fallback did and never\n  1090\t    silently. A composition that chooses `:trusted` has chosen to\n  1091\t    accept the publisher's lowering, and the outcome says so.\n  1092\t  The B6 fallback pair maps onto these: `verifying-fallback` is the\n  1093\t  `:verifying` policy applied to an H image selected in place of an\n  1094\t  unsupported R; `trusted-fallback` is `:trusted` applied the same way.\n  1095\t  The `:yin.ast/code` image needs no derivation under either policy: it\n  1096\t  is the tree, verified by content.\n  1097\t- **The name is verified.** Immediately after the manifest passes steps\n  1098\t  2 to 4, the linker compares `:yin.module/name` with the\n  1099\t  `:yin.link/name` it was resolved under; inequality is\n  1100\t  `:module-name-mismatch` naming both. A name-environment entry can\n  1101\t  therefore point only at a manifest that claims that name.\n  1102\t- `:yin.module/index` maps contract-pinned identities to their storage\n  1103\t  addresses, the H and R indexes of B6 section 6 carried beside the\n  1104\t  identities they resolve. It is merged into linker-local `:indexes`\n  1105\t  when the manifest is verified, never consulted from the wire.\n  1120\t\n  1121\tNames live outside the manifest. A name environment maps a module name\n  1122\tto a manifest address and is linker-local state in one of two shapes:\n  1123\t\n  1124\t- a plain map `{'my.lib :segment/...}`, for tests and single-process\n  1125\t  compositions, with the composition itself as the sole asserter;\n  1126\t- assertions read from a `dao.space` source under the policy below.\n  1127\t\n  1128\t**Fail-closed assertion policy.** This policy is an entry criterion for\n  1129\tM4: no linker may resolve a name from `dao.space` assertions before it is\n  1130\timplemented and tested.\n  1131\t\n  1132\t- An assertion is the datoms `[name :yin.module/manifest address]`,\n  1133\t  `[name :yin.module/asserted-by principal]`, and a **proof**,\n  1134\t  transacted together. A bare `:asserted-by` value is a string anyone\n  1135\t  can write and is never honored on its own; an assertion whose proof\n  1136\t  is absent or fails verification is `:unauthenticated` and discarded\n  1137\t  before counting. Two proof kinds are admitted, and the composition's\n  1138\t  `:authority` declares, per principal, which one it expects:\n  1139\t  - *Signature.* `[name :yin.module/signature sig]`, where `sig` signs\n  1140\t    the canonical bytes (`dao.jing/canonical-bytes`) of\n  1141\t    `{:name name :manifest address :asserted-by principal}` under the\n  1142\t    principal's public key. `:authority` holds, per principal, the key\n  1143\t    and a composition-supplied verify function; the linker calls the\n  1144\t    function and implements no cryptographic primitive itself (a host\n  1145\t    library sits behind the function, as `dao.jing` does for hashing).\n  1146\t    The signature is over content, so it survives every carrier and\n  1147\t    can be checked by any host that holds the key.\n  1148\t  - *Attested log.* The assertion stream is a one-writer log\n  1149\t    (`dao.stream.md`, *Concurrency*) whose writer the composition\n  1150\t    authenticated at attachment and bound to one principal; `:authority`\n  1151\t    names the stream's `:dao.stream/identity` beside that principal, and\n  1152\t    every assertion read from that identity, at the snapshot, counts as\n  1153\t    proven by it. The proof here is the composition's attachment\n  1154\t    discipline, and it holds only within the reach of that discipline:\n  1155\t    an assertion copied off that log onto another medium carries no\n  1156\t    proof and is `:unauthenticated` there. A composition that needs\n  1157\t    assertions to travel uses signatures.\n  1158\t  A principal declared with neither kind cannot assert anything.\n  1159\t- The linker's `:authority` names the accepted principals with their\n  1160\t  proof kind and material, and the **snapshot**: either a published\n  1161\t  index manifest address (`dao.space.index`) or a cursor position on\n  1162\t  the assertion stream. The linker reads exactly that snapshot;\n  1163\t  assertions appended afterwards do not exist for it until the\n  1164\t  composition advances the snapshot, which is an ordinary `link-state`\n  1165\t  rebuild, never an ambient re-read.\n  1166\t- At the snapshot, for one name: unauthenticated assertions and\n  1167\t  assertions by undeclared principals are discarded; retractions are\n  1168\t  honored only when they carry the same proof as the assertion they\n  1169\t  retract; the distinct manifest addresses remaining are counted.\n  1170\t  Exactly one resolves the name. Zero is `:absent`. More than one is\n  1171\t  `:ambiguous-name` naming every remaining address and asserter, and no\n  1172\t  address is chosen by recency, order, or any other rule.\n  1173\t- Provenance is data the outcome carries: a successful resolution names\n  1174\t  the asserter, the proof kind, and the snapshot under\n  1175\t  `:yin.link/provenance` so a receiver can record which claim it acted\n  1176\t  on.\n  1177\t\n  1178\tAn entry is a claim. The manifest it names is verified, and its name is\n  1179\tverified against the entry (section 8.1). Which principals a composition\n  1180\tdeclares, and how it obtains and rotates their keys, is the composition's\n  1181\t(section 12); what the linker does with a declared principal's assertion\n  1182\tis fixed above.\n  1183\t\n  1184\t### 8.3 What `yin.vm.module` becomes\n  1185\t\n  1186\tThe namespace keeps its shape and changes its contents.\n  1187\t\n  1188\t| Today                        | After                                       |\n  1189\t|------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n  1190\t| `register-module r name fns` | `register-host-module r name fns profiles`  |\n  1478\t- **Unrecognized outcomes.** `dao.stream.md` OD-1 proposes that a\n  1479\t  consumer treat an unrecognized outcome as refused and not retryable.\n  1480\t  The stepped core follows that rule once OD-1 is accepted; until then\n  1481\t  it classifies unknown content-server answers as `transport-error`, as\n  1482\t  `dao.stream.observe/step` does.\n  1483\t\n  1484\t## 13. Reconciliation record\n  1485\t\n  1486\t### 13.1 Revision r3 (round 2, `gpt-6-sol`)\n  1487\t\n  1488\tThe round-2 review verified round-1 items 1, 7, 9a to 9e, and 10 as\n  1489\tclosed and marked seven items partial plus one ordering defect. Each,\n  1490\tand what r3 did:\n  1491\t\n  1492\t| Item                              | Resolution                        |\n  1493\t|-----------------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n  1494\t| Wire request example carried both | Agreed. Two mutually exclusive    |\n  1495\t| name and identity; refusal table  | examples; both-or-neither is      |\n  1496\t| lacked `:invalid-request`         | `:invalid-request`; reason added  |\n  1497\t|                                   | to the table at step 0 (4.3, 6.3).|\n  1498\t| Module-local definitions flagged  | Agreed. `:definitions-fn` yields  |\n  1499\t| as `:undeclared-free`             | the constant-key definition set,  |\n  1500\t|                                   | subtracted before the join; also  |\n  1501\t|                                   | gives an early `:export-missing`  |\n  1502\t|                                   | (4.1, 4.2 step 5a, 5.x records).  |\n  1503\t| VM-local counters collide on one  | Agreed. Link id is `[origin       |\n  1504\t| response stream                   | counter]`; origin minted per task |\n  1505\t|                                   | by the scheduler owning the pair; |\n  1506\t|                                   | multiplexed schedulers need       |\n  1507\t|                                   | distinct prefixes (7.2 step 3).   |\n  1508\t| Exported closures carry child     | Agreed. `linked` is load, lift to |\n  1509\t| code coordinates                  | UCF closure markers relative to   |\n  1510\t|                                   | the image identity, lower against |\n  1511\t|                                   | the parent's alias column or      |\n  1512\t|                                   | offset (7.3). Divergence: export  |\n  1513\t|                                   | relocation chosen over execution  |\n  1514\t|                                   | in the owning child, which would  |\n  1515\t|                                   | be a cross-VM function call.      |\n  1516\t| `:fallback :verifying` could      | Agreed. `:derivation` policy is   |\n  1517\t| accept an unverified claim        | `:verifying` or `:trusted`;       |\n  1518\t|                                   | verifying fetches the tree and    |\n  1519\t|                                   | re-lowers under the exact         |\n  1520\t|                                   | per-format profile or refuses     |\n  1521\t|                                   | `:unverified-derivation`; it      |\n  1522\t|                                   | never downgrades (8.1, 6.2).      |\n  1523\t| Bare `:asserted-by` honored       | Agreed. Proof required: a         |\n  1524\t|                                   | signature over canonical bytes    |\n  1525\t|                                   | verified by a composition-        |\n  1526\t|                                   | supplied function, or an attested |\n  1527\t|                                   | one-writer log bound to the       |\n  1528\t|                                   | principal at attachment; unproven |\n  1529\t|                                   | is `:unauthenticated`; retraction |\n  1530\t|                                   | needs matching proof (8.2). Key   |\n  1531\t|                                   | management stays open (12).       |\n  1532\t| Host modules implied a sandbox    | Agreed. The host module is named  |\n  1533\t|                                   | the trusted composition boundary; |\n  1534\t|                                   | the profile check is a declaration|\n  1535\t|                                   | check; run-time confinement is    |\n  1536\t|                                   | the capability-token discipline,  |\n  1537\t|                                   | outside the linker (8.3).         |\n  1538\t| Contract checked after row-local  | Agreed; r2 ordering defect. The   |\n  1539\t| validation                        | contract check is step 0, before  |\n  1540\t|                                   | any fetch or validator, and is    |\n  1541\t|                                   | repeated against the manifest's   |\n  1542\t|                                   | contract before any image fetch   |\n  1543\t|                                   | (4.2 step 0, 8.1).                |\n  1544\t\n  1545\t### 13.2 Revision r2 (round 1, `gpt-5.6-sol`)\n  1546\t\n  1547\tEach finding of the 2026-09-24 adversarial review, and what r2 did.\n  1548\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
