{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '175,235p;575,615p'\"","aggregated_output":"   175\t```clojure\n   176\t[[:closure [x] 6]           ; mnemonic, params, resolved body pc\n   177\t [:push]\n   178\t [:const 10]                ; §2.5 literal: data only\n   179\t [:call 1 false]            ; argc, tail?\n   180\t …]\n   181\t```\n   182\t\n   183\tRules, each replacing heavier machinery from the attributed-map record:\n   184\t\n   185\t- **pc is the index.** No entity ids and no attribute names appear anywhere\n   186\t  in the form: position fixes meaning, the §2.4 table fixes shape, and a\n   187\t  query over code is a query over tuples of the vector, not over EAV.\n   188\t- **Saturation.** Defaults the loader applies are materialized in the tuple —\n   189\t  `:gensym` absent prefix becomes `\"id\"`, `:ffi-call` absent argc becomes\n   190\t  `0`, `:stream-make` absent buffer becomes the default capacity — so a batch\n   191\t  that omitted a defaulted operand and one that stated it canonicalize\n   192\t  identically, because they execute identically.\n   193\t- **Refs are resolved pcs**, exactly as the loader resolves them.\n   194\t- **The header folds away.** `:yin.code/length` is `(count vector)`,\n   195\t  `:yin.code/type :segment` is implied by the form. Provenance\n   196\t  (`:yin.code/derived-from`, every `:yin.code/source`), entity ids, batch\n   197\t  order, `t`, `m`, and `:yin.code/hash` itself are excluded — the address is\n   198\t  computed *over* the vector, which therefore cannot contain it without\n   199\t  hashing the hash being verified.\n   200\t- **Admissible slots.** Each tuple is its mnemonic plus exactly the operands\n   201\t  the §2.4 table gives it, in the table's order. A batch that resolves to\n   202\t  anything else — a mnemonic outside the table, an operand the mnemonic's\n   203\t  tuple has no slot for, a segment-entity attribute beyond the folded and\n   204\t  excluded set — is **non-canonicalizable**, and canonicalization refuses\n   205\t  naming the instruction (the lifted value is never minted; the refusal is\n   206\t  `:yin.k/non-portable` with `:yin.k/kind :non-canonicalizable`).\n   207\t- **Ordering: none is imposed.** The vector's order is its positions;\n   208\t  canonicalization sorts nothing and compares no operand against another.\n   209\t  Literal operands are values and hash under the same encoder as everything\n   210\t  else, so equal literals address equally however their maps were built.\n   211\t  The first draft's `[:segment …]`-versus-`[0 …]` triple sort — which throws\n   212\t  `Keyword cannot be cast to Number` in the very host it must run on — has\n   213\t  no successor here because there is no sort at all.\n   214\t\n   215\t**The address** is `(dao.jing/segment-key vector)` — a\n   216\t`:segment/sha256-<hex>` keyword. The vector *is* the resolved\n   217\tinterpretation, so r2's collision-freedom argument carries over unchanged\n   218\tand needs only restating: canonicalization and execution read the same\n   219\tvalue by construction; there is nothing left to collide. The address fixes\n   220\tthe resolved instruction stream — mnemonics, operands, control graph,\n   221\tsaturations — and nothing else.\n   222\t\n   223\t**What equal addresses do and do not mean.** Two segments address equally\n   224\t**iff** their loader-resolved interpretations are equal; this includes\n   225\tsegments that differ only in entity ids, batch order, provenance, or omitted\n   226\tdefaulted operands. No equivalence beyond that is claimed: different pc\n   227\tlayouts, different `:gensym` prefixes, different but *equivalent* instruction\n   228\tsequences, and compiler-renamed artifacts address **differently**. The\n   229\taddress is a fingerprint of one instruction stream, not a quotient of a\n   230\tsemantics.\n   231\t\n   232\tThe canonical encoding beneath `segment-key` is transitional\n   233\t(`dao.jing.md`, *Open items*). UCF inherits that limit unchanged: addresses\n   234\tare portable exactly as far as `dao.jing` addresses are, and when the pinned\n   235\tbyte encoding lands, `:yin.code/hash` values change with every other minted\n   575\tliteral is re-wrapped one level down and peeled back exactly one level on\n   576\tdecode. No rule asks whether a map \"looks like\" data; no decoder decision\n   577\tdepends on program content. Scalar and collection values pass through only\n   578\tbecause no marker is ever a non-map, which the closed tag table fixes.\n   579\t\n   580\tMap keys are values and are encoded as such — inside `:yin.k/entries`\n   581\talternately with their values, and nowhere else; the frame's own `:yin.k/env`\n   582\tkeys are the program's symbols and are structural, not encoded values.\n   583\t\n   584\t**Decoding validates, independent of hashing.** A hash check confirms what\n   585\twas encoded; it does not confirm the encoding is well formed. The decoder\n   586\twalks the grammar and rejects, as `:yin.k/undecodable` naming the path: a map\n   587\twith no `:yin.k/tag` at an encoded-value position (impossible from a\n   588\tconforming encoder); a tag outside the closed set; a marker missing its\n   589\trequired keys; a `:yin.k/ref` whose address is absent from the value table; a\n   590\tref graph that is cyclic *through ref data* (content-addressed table entries\n   591\tcannot mint such a cycle — a cycle can only be written by hand, and it is\n   592\trejected); and a value table entry not referenced by anything reachable.\n   593\tMalformed-but-correctly-hashed input fails here, before any lowering.\n   594\t\n   595\t### 7.5.2 Primitives: a name is a binding, not a meaning\n   596\t\n   597\t§1.1 admits host functions into the environment because `:var` resolves a\n   598\tprimitive symbol to the function value and a program may then bind it\n   599\t(`(def f +)`). At lift time the emitter recognizes such a value by reverse\n   600\tlookup in its own `:primitives` map — identity on the function object. The\n   601\tfirst draft treated the recovered symbol as the whole story. It is not:\n   602\t\n   603\t- one function object may sit under **several names**, making reverse lookup\n   604\t  ambiguous;\n   605\t- two composition-supplied primitive maps may bind the **same symbol to\n   606\t  different functions** — name presence at the resumer proves nothing about\n   607\t  behavior;\n   608\t- identity stability says nothing about **purity or captured host state**.\n   609\t\n   610\tUCF therefore binds every portable function to a **primitive profile**,\n   611\tpublished data, and the binding is checked on both ends:\n   612\t\n   613\t```clojure\n   614\t;; in :yin.k/requires — computed, not hand-written (§7.6.1)\n   615\t:yin.k/primitives {+\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
