{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '415,450p;800,840p;915,955p;1188,1218p'; nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '45,105p'\"","aggregated_output":"   415\t   construction and would license a binding that no longer holds. The\n   416\t   host-policy `fetch` (section 6.4), which runs in one process with the\n   417\t   receiver in hand, runs both halves before returning.\n   418\t6. **Return the verified image.** `{:status :ok :format f :identity i\n   419\t   :address a :value v :obligations [...]}` plus `:parts {address value}`\n   420\t   for a multi-part format and `:derivation {...}` where a manifest\n   421\t   supplied one (section 8.1). Nothing partially verified is ever\n   422\t   returned.\n   423\t\n   424\t### 4.3 Refusal vocabulary\n   425\t\n   426\tEvery refusal is `{:status :refused :reason r}` merged with the step's own\n   427\tevidence, exactly as `yin.vm.linker/refused` builds it.\n   428\t\n   429\t| Reason                  | Step   | Meaning                                  |\n   430\t|-------------------------|--------|------------------------------------------|\n   431\t| `:invalid-request`      | 0      | a request outside the closed key set of  |\n   432\t|                         |        | section 6.3, or with a non-portable value|\n   433\t| `:absent`               | 1 or 2 | no index entry, or no payload at the     |\n   434\t|                         |        | address (root or any part)               |\n   435\t| `:address-mismatch`     | 2      | payload does not hash to its address     |\n   436\t| `:parts-limit`          | 2      | a composition bound on parts, depth, or  |\n   437\t|                         |        | bytes was exceeded                       |\n   438\t| `:hash-mismatch`        | 3      | root value does not match the identity   |\n   439\t| `:descriptor-defect`    | 2 or 4 | row-local or whole-value validation      |\n   440\t|                         |        | failed                                   |\n   441\t| `:contract-mismatch`    | 0      | request or manifest names a contract the |\n   442\t|                         |        | format record does not implement         |\n   443\t| `:undeclared-free`      | 5a     | a free name has no declaration in the    |\n   444\t|                         |        | manifest that delivered the image        |\n   445\t| `:unresolved-free`      | 5b     | an obligation is not discharged by an    |\n   446\t|                         |        | equal profile or manifest                |\n   447\t| `:shadowed-free`        | 5b     | an obligation's name is bound by the     |\n   448\t|                         |        | receiver's free env or store             |\n   449\t| `:unsupported-format`   | 0      | the receiver holds no kernel or format   |\n   450\t|                         |        | record for the requested format          |\n   800\t    L->>S: append link response {id, :ok image, obligations}\n   801\t    S->>S: match id; discharge obligations (5b)\n   802\t    S->>S: child task: loading -> running -> validated\n   803\t    S->>S: registry value += {'foo manifest, bindings}\n   804\t    S-->>P: 'foo\n   805\t  end\n   806\t```\n   807\t\n   808\tThe steps, as rules:\n   809\t\n   810\t1. **The effect is unchanged.** `require` still returns\n   811\t   `{:effect :module/require :module 'foo}`. Nothing upstream of the\n   812\t   engine learns anything.\n   813\t2. **The handler checks the registry value first.** A manifest already\n   814\t   linked in `(:modules state)` answers now, as `next` answers `ok` when\n   815\t   the value is there. This is not the dictionary lookup surviving: the\n   816\t   registry holds manifests and bindings produced by an earlier child\n   817\t   evaluation, or by the composition's host-module declaration (section\n   818\t   8.3). A module currently installing (section 7.3) is neither linked\n   819\t   nor absent: the requiring task joins that install's waiter set.\n   820\t3. **A miss mints a cursor, then parks in the request state.** In this\n   821\t   order: the handler mints a `:dao.stream/newest` cursor on the link\n   822\t   response stream; then it builds the wait entry in its first state:\n   823\t\n   824\t   ```clojure\n   825\t   {:reason    :link-request\n   826\t    :link-id   [:t0 7]                 ; [origin counter], below\n   827\t    :envelope  {:yin.link/id [:t0 7] :yin.link/name 'foo\n   828\t                :yin.link/format :yin.debruijn.code\n   829\t                :yin.link/contract \"b1\"}\n   830\t    :request   {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\n   831\t    :response  {:dao.stream/identity ... :dao.stream/descriptor ...}\n   832\t    :cursor    <the kept cursor minted above>}\n   833\t   ```\n   834\t\n   835\t   Minting before appending is the `dao.stream.md` *Cursors* rule for\n   836\t   observing events caused by an operation: a response that lands\n   837\t   before the cursor exists would otherwise be skipped. The entry is\n   838\t   registers, resource ids, and plain data only; no handle and no\n   839\t   closure is attached.\n   840\t\n   915\tstateDiagram-v2\n   916\t  [*] --> loading: 5b discharged\n   917\t  loading --> running: kernel loader accepted the image\n   918\t  loading --> refused: loader defect\n   919\t  running --> parked: child effect blocks (stream, ffi, require)\n   920\t  parked --> running: child wait satisfied\n   921\t  running --> validated: halt; every export bound in child store\n   922\t  running --> refused: halt; :export-missing, or error\n   923\t  validated --> linked: bindings published to registry value\n   924\t  linked --> [*]\n   925\t  refused --> [*]\n   926\t```\n   927\t\n   928\tRules:\n   929\t\n   930\t- **The child is a task, not a call.** The scheduler holds\n   931\t  `:installs {name {:phase p :vm child :waiters [task-ids] :parent\n   932\t  link-id}}`. The child is a VM of the same backend with a fresh,\n   933\t  isolated store containing nothing of any other task's state (UCF\n   934\t  section 7.6.2 applied to loading), its own wait-set, and the parent's\n   935\t  registry value as its module view. The scheduler steps it in its\n   936\t  ordinary round beside every other task; nothing about it runs inside\n   937\t  wait restoration.\n   938\t- **The requiring task waits on the install, not on the linker.** Once\n   939\t  the child starts, the parent's entry becomes\n   940\t  `{:reason :install :name 'foo}`, and any other task that requires\n   941\t  `'foo` meanwhile joins `:waiters`. No task is restored until the\n   942\t  install reaches `linked` or `refused`.\n   943\t- **`running` may park.** A module body that reads a stream, calls FFI,\n   944\t  or requires another module parks the child exactly as any task parks;\n   945\t  a transitive require emits its own link request under its own id\n   946\t  (section 7.4). The parent is parked on `:install` and is unaffected;\n   947\t  the scheduler keeps stepping every other task. There is nothing to\n   948\t  deadlock on except a cycle, which section 7.4 refuses.\n   949\t- **`validated` requires halt plus exports.** The child must reach halt\n   950\t  (not error, not an abandoned park), and every name under\n   951\t  `:yin.module/exports` must be a key of the child's store. A missing\n   952\t  export is `:export-missing`; an error is the error. Nothing from a\n   953\t  refused child's store is published.\n   954\t- **`linked` relocates the exports, then updates the registry.** A\n   955\t  child's store holds closures whose code coordinates are the child's:\n  1188\t| Today                        | After                                       |\n  1189\t|------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|\n  1190\t| `register-module r name fns` | `register-host-module r name fns profiles`  |\n  1191\t|                              | (rules below); no code fetched, because the |\n  1192\t|                              | host is the boundary                        |\n  1193\t| `resolve-module r sym`       | unchanged signature; reads `:bindings`      |\n  1194\t| `require-handler`            | registry hit answers; installing joins the  |\n  1195\t|                              | waiter set; miss mints, parks, and emits    |\n  1196\t|                              | (section 7.2); never throws for absence     |\n  1197\t| `register-effect-handler`    | unchanged                                   |\n  1198\t| `stream-module`              | a host module; `register-stream-module`     |\n  1199\t|                              | calls `register-host-module`                |\n  1200\t| `default-registry`           | unchanged                                   |\n  1201\t| (new) `link-module r m d s`  | the `linked` transition of section 7.3      |\n  1202\t\n  1203\t**`register-host-module` enforces UCF profile classes.** Every binding\n  1204\tmust carry a profile in the shape of `yin.vm/primitive-profiles`, and\n  1205\tregistration refuses (throws, as a host assembly defect detected before\n  1206\tany operation runs) unless the profile's class is `:pure`, or\n  1207\t`:effectful` with a declared, non-empty `:yin.k/effects` set, and\n  1208\t`:yin.k/host-state` is `:none`. A `:host`-class function, a function\n  1209\twith undeclared host state, or a binding with no profile is refused. An\n  1210\t`:effectful` export must return plain effect data for the engine to\n  1211\tinterpret; it may not perform IO. `stream/make` is exactly such a pure\n  1212\teffect constructor, and the engine's `:make-stream` capability remains\n  1213\tthe explicit boundary that performs the creation. A host module is\n  1214\tentered as an already-linked manifest with `:yin.module/tree` absent,\n  1215\t`:yin.module/derivations {}`, and its exports listed under\n  1216\t`:yin.module/primitives` by profile address.\n  1217\t\n  1218\t**The host module is the trusted composition boundary, and the check\n    45\t\n    46\tThe Universal Continuation Format (UCF) is the **lift** of that local record\n    47\tinto a self-describing value that any conforming engine can **lower** back\n    48\tinto its own registers, and the discipline that governs who may lower it.\n    49\tThree roles participate, and they are stream roles, not parties to a call:\n    50\t\n    51\t| Role | Does | Holds |\n    52\t|---|---|---|\n    53\t| **Emitter** | lifts a parked configuration into a UCF value and appends it to a medium | nothing, once appended |\n    54\t| **Carrier** | any stream, file, DHT, or socket that moves the value; sees opaque data | nothing |\n    55\t| **Resumer** | reads the value, satisfies its declared context, obtains custody, lowers, runs | custody, for the duration of a lease |\n    56\t\n    57\tThree consequences are load-bearing. First, **a UCF value is content**: it has\n    58\ta content address under `dao.jing`, equal values converge on one address, and\n    59\tcarrying it twice is harmless (`dao.jing.md`, *Materialization rule*). Second,\n    60\t**a UCF value on a stream is not a claim**: a stream has no privileged reader\n    61\t(`datom.world.md`, *Streams*), so emission transfers nothing. Custody is\n    62\tarbitrated above the stream, by leases over datoms (§7.7), exactly where\n    63\t`dao.stream.md` *Explicitly Absent* says a take belongs. Third, **content is\n    64\tnot occurrence**: the same parked computation can be encoded more than once\n    65\t(sharing thresholds differ, §7.5.3), so the encodings address differently,\n    66\twhile remaining one *checkpoint occurrence*. Content identity is for\n    67\tdeduplication and materialization; occurrence identity is what custody names\n    68\t(§7.3.4, §7.7). Conflating them was a defect of the first draft; this draft\n    69\tnever does.\n    70\t\n    71\tThe reference engine for every rule below is `yin.vm.semantic`. Its\n    72\tconfiguration ⟨C, E, S, K⟩ with C = ⟨seg, pc, val, St⟩ (`yin.vm.semantic.md`\n    73\t§4.1) is the **canonical CESK**. A foreign engine conforms by lifting into and\n    74\tlowering from this configuration, never by extending it.\n    75\t\n    76\t## §7.2 Vocabulary and envelope\n    77\t\n    78\tUCF facts live under `:yin.k/*` (K for the continuation component of CESK).\n    79\tA UCF value is one open map dispatching on `:yin.k/type`; a consumer ignores\n    80\tqualified keys it does not understand, as for every DaoStream envelope. The\n    81\tvalue must survive the host codec structurally unchanged: no function, host\n    82\tobject, or live handle anywhere inside it (`dao.stream.md`, *Envelopes*).\n    83\tEvery value it contains is encoded in the disjoint tagged grammar of §7.5 —\n    84\tnever a bare marker map.\n    85\t\n    86\t```clojure\n    87\t{:yin.k/type        :yin.k/continuation\n    88\t :yin.k/contract    {:yin.code/contract \"v2\" :yin.k/version 0}   ; §7.3.3\n    89\t :yin.k/id          :segment/sha256-…          ; §7.3.4  content address of this map minus :yin.k/id\n    90\t :yin.k/occurrence  :yin.k/o-…                  ; §7.7    the checkpoint occurrence — the lease subject\n    91\t :yin.k/origin      {:yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/o-…                        ; predecessor, or absent on a first park\n    92\t                    :dao.lease/lease   :dao.lease/l-…                     ; the grant it ran under\n    93\t                    :yin.k/emitter     <attribution>}\n    94\t :yin.k/arbitration {:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …}     ; §7.7.3  the authority medium, named in the value\n    95\t :yin.k/frame       {…}                        ; §7.4  the canonical CESK at a safepoint\n    96\t :yin.k/values      {…}                        ; §7.5.3  shared subvalues, by content address\n    97\t :yin.k/cells       {…}                        ; §7.5.3  cursor cells: logical identity + position\n    98\t :yin.k/scheduler   {…}                        ; §7.6.3  fresh-name state and referenced parked records\n    99\t :yin.k/requires    {…}                        ; §7.6  dependency closure, with discovery status\n   100\t :yin.k/store       {…}                        ; §7.6.2  the store slice restored as an isolated store\n   101\t :yin.k/carried     [ … ]                      ; §7.3.4  canonical instruction vectors shipped inline\n   102\t :yin.k/policy      :yin.k/exclusive}          ; §7.7  or :yin.k/fork\n   103\t```\n   104\t\n   105\tThe parts answer four questions a resumer must be able to ask of the data\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
